  "image_name": "44221_2026_600_Fig1_HTML.webp",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two electrochemical nitrate-reduction strategies for wastewater treatment powered by renewable energy. At the top, a schematic shows an electro‑NO3RR cathode converting nitrate in water through nitrite and surface intermediates to ammonia, which can then be captured as ammonium chloride. The main comparison is between a conventional hydrogen-atom-transfer route and the authors’ proton-coupled electron transfer route.\n\nIn the middle, previous work is depicted as suffering from competing reactions. Weak hydrogen-bonding around the catalyst surface appears to favor proton reduction and hydrogen evolution (HER), so electrons and H species are diverted to H2 instead of selectively driving nitrate toward NH3. The cartoon of the interface suggests disordered water and poor proton delivery, which increases HER competition.\n\nAt the bottom, the proposed system shows the opposite behavior. A strong hydrogen-bond network and a defined H+ transport channel organize interfacial water near the catalyst, enabling proton-coupled electron transfer to nitrate-derived intermediates. This suppresses the competing hydrogen evolution pathway and promotes the desired conversion from nitrate to ammonia. Overall, the figure argues that engineering the interfacial hydrogen-bond network improves selectivity and efficiency for electrochemical nitrate removal and ammonia production.",
